@@ -194,7 +194,18 @@
        the label step 4.8:1 -> 5.6:1, which is the one that was genuinely thin.
        The neutrals carry TEXT in 638 places and a background or border in 6,
        which is why re-hueing them is a type change and not a surface one. */
-    --color-text:#1B2A28;
+    /* ---- THE INK IS THE DESIGN'S OWN, TO THE BYTE (owner-reported 24 Aug
+       2026: "the html has a very beautiful design on how they use the black
+       fonts ... but HaTi uses grey fonts instead which is not striking") ----
+       The 22 Aug retune re-hued this ramp onto the brand's green-black family
+       and got the HUE right and the DEPTH wrong: every step landed a shade
+       lighter than the design's. MEASURED against tokens.css, step for step —
+       primary #1B2A28 against its #0E1A18, label #5F6D6B against #54635F,
+       quiet #93A09E against #8A9997 — so the whole product read one notch
+       softer than the reference on every screen at once. They are the design's
+       values now, which is a change of DEPTH and not of hue: contrast on white
+       goes 14.9:1 to 17.4:1 on the primary, and nothing gets lighter. */
+    --color-text:#0E1A18;
     /* The one-word accent IS step 600 of the ramp — the two were the same
        colour typed twice, which is how a theme moves one and forgets the other. */
     --color-accent:var(--color-accent-600);
@@ -232,8 +243,8 @@
        against 39 text uses, so it is the one step in this ramp that is not a
        type token, and the rulebook's "re-hueing the neutrals is safe" argument
        does not cover it. */
